why 1 3/4 PARALLEL_OCTAVES Tenor and bass go from a unison G to an octave E♭, with the tenor's B♭ in between. For Bach the skip separates them; strict exercises still count it.
why 1 3/4 OVERLAP The tenor rises to E♭, above where the alto was, while the alto leaps up to G. They move up together.
why 3 3/4 SIMILAR_MOTION All voices rise into ii°⁶ before the half cadence, the bass by a fourth. Each line moves by its own interval.
why 3 3/4 LEAPS The tenor climbs A–C–D–E♭ toward the half cadence. Its ends form a tritone, but the line moves mostly by step.
why 9 1/2 RANGE The tenor climbs F–G–A alongside the alto and then drops an octave; the high A lasts one eighth.
why 11 5/8 MELODIC The bass leaps up a seventh, G to F, inside the G minor chord before falling to E♭. It is the step G–F moved up an octave, which keeps the bass from going too low.
why 12 1/2 SIMILAR_MOTION The piece ends on G major, a Picardy third. Every voice falls into it: the seventh C to B, the alto's F♯ to D and the bass to G.
